      "explanation": [
        "Main idea: bloß means only or merely, indicating that a quantity, value or reason is small or limited. In colloquial speech it is close to nur.",
        "Bloß also adds emphasis to questions and warnings: Wie konnte das bloß passieren? (How on earth could that happen?), Lass das bloß! (Just leave that alone!). In this way it expresses alarm or a warning.",
        "As an adjective, bloß means bare: mit bloßen Händen (with bare hands). It then takes normal adjective endings."
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "de": "Ich wollte dich bloß fragen, ob du Zeit hast.",
          "lv": "I just wanted to ask whether you have time."
        },
        {
          "de": "Das ist bloß ein Missverständnis.",
          "lv": "It is merely a misunderstanding."
        },
        {
          "de": "Wie konnte das bloß passieren?",
          "lv": "How on earth could that happen?"
        },
        {
          "de": "Sie öffnete die Kiste mit bloßen Händen.",
          "lv": "She opened the box with her bare hands."
        }
      ],
      "comparison": [
        {
          "word": "nur",
          "meaning": "only",
          "example": "Ich habe nur zehn Euro. – I only have ten euros."
        },
        {
          "word": "allein",
          "meaning": "only; alone",
          "example": "Allein das zählt. – That alone counts."
        }
      ],
      "tip": [
        "In everyday speech bloß and nur can often replace each other.",
        "In commands and questions, bloß adds emotion."
      ],
      "important": [
        "Bloß sounds more colloquial than nur.",
        "As an adjective, bloß takes endings: bloße Hände, mit bloßen Händen."
      ],
